==============================================================================
PART 5.  THE RETARGETED COVERAGE LEMMA (sibling interface, e42d785)
==============================================================================
  Construction B consumes exactly one property of n: gamma(n) >=
  Gamma(p,n), plus the scale side-condition (H0). Nothing about the
  continued fraction of L is used by it -- so the lemma this session
  owes reduces to a density statement.

  (1) The threshold. Gamma(p,n) rises monotonically to its limit;
      at kappa = n/1.585^p = 1.05 (the top of the target window):

          p   Gamma@1.05  delta threshold
          6      3.17460          0.16934
          8      3.38921          0.14472
         10      3.54395          0.12932
         12      3.61237          0.12306
         16      3.66822          0.11820
         20      3.68005          0.11719
         30      3.68297          0.11694
         60      3.68301          0.11694
        120      3.68301          0.11694
        400      3.68301          0.11694

      sup over p = 6..2000 of Gamma(p, 1.05*1.585^p) = 3.683012,
      so it suffices that   ceil(nL) - nL <= 0.116939
      -- a fixed density condition, with NO p-dependence at all.

  (2) LEMMA D (elementary, unconditional, no continued-fraction theory
      beyond one exact number). Let theta = 8 - 5L = 0.0751874964 > 0.
      Since 5L = 8 - theta, stepping n -> n+5 decreases {nL} by exactly
      theta (mod 1). The 15 points n = N, N+5, ..., N+70 therefore lie
      theta apart along a full turn (14*theta = 1.052625 >= 1),
      so ANY arc of length > theta contains one of them. The target arc
      {nL} in [1 - 0.116939, 1) has length 0.116939 > theta.
      Hence: among any 71 consecutive integers there is an n
      with ceil(nL) - nL <= 0.116939.
      Brute force over n = 1..3,000,000: longest run of consecutive
      integers ALL failing the condition is 11 (proved bound 70).

      NEGATIVE CONTROL: the sweep's guarantee must FAIL for an arc
      shorter than theta -- otherwise the threshold is doing no work.
      The 15 sweep points sit exactly theta apart, so an arc of length
      theta/2 can be placed between two of them and missed entirely:
      399/399 starting points N give an arc of length theta/2 = 0.03759375
      containing NO sweep point. Above theta the guarantee holds; below
      it, it genuinely does not.

  (3) THEOREM D. The window [1.585^p, 1.05*1.585^p] holds 0.05*1.585^p integers,
      which reaches 71 at p = 16. So for every p >= 16, Lemma D puts an n
      satisfying the hypothesis inside the window -- UNCONDITIONALLY,
      with no effective irrationality measure and no hypothesis on the
      partial quotients of log2 3. For p < 16 the window is checked directly.

